The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this division, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Asset acquisition means the various methods by which the city acquires assets. The term "asset acquisition" includes, but is not limited to, the following methods:
(1) Purchase;
(2) Donation;
(3) Lease/purchase;
(4) Trade-in;
(5) Forfeiture;
(6) Condemnation;
(7) Internal/external construction;
(8) Transfers from other governments; or
(9) Any other method which transfers title of any property to the city.
Asset capitalization amount means the city will capitalize purchased assets at acquisition cost plus costs incurred in preparing the asset for use. The city will recognize acquisition costs based on individual unit prices.
Capital asset means any real or personal property acquired by the city which has an estimated useful life of three or more years with an acquisition value of $10,000.00 or more. The term "capital asset" includes:
(1) Land;
(2) Improvements to land;
(3) Easements;
(4) Buildings;
(5) Building improvements;
(6) Vehicles;
(7) Machinery;
(8) Equipment;
(9) Works of art and historical treasures;
(10) Infrastructure; and
(11) All other tangible or intangible assets that are used in the operations of the government.
Generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP) reporting means all assets capitalized under this division shall be included in the financial statements issued by the city and in the annual external audit.
Gifts/donations means the city capitalizes all equipment acquired through donations at fair market value on the date of the transaction; provided such gift or donation meets the capitalization threshold of this division.
(1) If the equipment is new and the donor can furnish an invoice, the invoice would determine the fair market value.
(2) If the equipment is used or no information is available regarding the cost of new equipment on the date of acquisition, an appraisal will be conducted to establish the capitalization amount.
Leased assets means the city capitalizes assets acquired under capital lease; provided they meet the capitalization threshold and a buy-out option is included in the lease agreement.
(1) The capital lease must meet accounting standards for capitalization purposes.
(2) For capital leases, where the title of the asset will ultimately be transferred to the city, the asset will be capitalized at the net present value of future minimum lease payments.
(3) The city does not capitalize assets it acquired under operating leases.
Property. Property is divided into several classes, including:
(1) Real property. The term "real property" means land and whatever is attached to the land that cannot be readily removed, such as buildings and permanent improvements to the land. The term "real property" includes infrastructures.
(2) Personal property. The term "personal property" means property that is movable and further classified as:
a. Tangible. The term "tangible personal property" means property that is moveable, such as:
1. Furniture;
2. Machinery;
3. Automobiles; or
4. Works of art.
b. Intangible. The term "intangible personal property" means the right of ownership in property such as:
1. Bonds;
2. Notes;
3. Contracts;
4. Computer software;
5. Programs; and
6. Proprietary assets that are created or purchased and owned by the city.
(Ord. No. 06-11-05, 11-21-2006)

297:
(a) Land.
(1) The city will capitalize all costs incurred to acquire land, without regard to a dollar threshold, and place it in use. The acquisition costs of land should include:
a. The purchase price;
b. Ancillary charges;
c. The assumption of any liens or mortgages on the property; and
d. Improvements made to the land that are permanent in nature.
(2) Examples of ancillary charges to be included in the capitalization costs are:
a. Legal and title fees;
b. Closing costs;
c. Appraisal and negotiation fees;
d. Surveying fees;
e. Site preparation fees;
f. Demolition costs;
g. Architect and accounting fees;
h. Insurance premiums during construction phase; and
i. Transportation charges.
(b) Improvements other than buildings (IMP). The city classifies improvements to land with limited lives, such as fences, parking lots, and walkways, as land improvements. These improvements will be depreciated over their estimated useful lives.
(c) Buildings (BLD).
(1) If the city purchases a building, the capitalized cost should include:
a. The purchase price; and
b. Other incidental expenses incurred at the time of acquisition.
(2) If the building is constructed, the capitalized cost should include material, labor, supervision, and overhead, or the contract price, including costs such as:
a. Permits and licenses;
b. Architectural and engineering fees;
c. Insurance;
d. Title costs; and
e. Interest incurred on tax exempt debt.
(d) Building improvements. The city capitalizes costs of improvements to a building if the following criteria are met:
(1) The expenditures increase the service potential of the building; and
(2) The total improvement costs, including the contract price and engineering, architectural, and attorney's fees, etc., meet the capitalization threshold of this division.
(3) Items considered as improvements include:
a. Ramps, fire escapes, truck doors or other appurtenances;
b. Modifications to comply with fire, health, or safety codes; and
c. Conversion of unusable to useable floor space and upgrade of the space.
(4) Repairs to a building are not considered an improvement unless they extend the useful life of the structure or increase the value of the structure (i.e., a betterment). The city considers ordinary repairs as operating costs.
(e) Construction in progress (CIP). Construction in progress includes the accumulation of ongoing project costs that increase the value or life of the asset. Upon project completion, the construction account in progress will be closed out and costs will be capitalized into the appropriate asset category (e.g., infrastructure, land improvement, building, etc.).
(f) Moveable equipment (EQP). Expenditures for individual items or pieces of equipment meeting the capitalization threshold shall be capitalized consistent with the designations of personal property, both tangible and intangible, as outlined in section 2-386.
(g) Infrastructure. Long-lived capital assets that normally are stationary in nature and normally can be preserved for a significantly greater number of years than most capital assets shall be capitalized based on the capitalization threshold requirement of this division. Examples of infrastructure include:
(1) Roads;
(2) Bridges;
(3) Drainage systems; and
(4) Sidewalks.
(Ord. No. 06-11-05, 11-21-2006)

